---
pagination:
  data: legacyRedirects
  size: 1
  alias: legacy
permalink: "{{ legacy.from }}"
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#- One stub per entry in src/_data/legacyRedirects.js — see that file for why
    they exist and why a server-side 301 is still the better answer.

    Excluded from collections so the sitemap advertises only the real URLs; a
    sitemap that lists a redirect asks a crawler to index the wrong address.
    `noindex, follow` says the same thing in the page's own head, and the
    canonical names the destination, so a crawler that arrives here has three
    consistent instructions.

    No `| url` on either destination. EleventyHtmlBasePlugin already rewrites
    `href` AND the `content` of a meta refresh for pathPrefix, so filtering
    them as well ships /<site-name>/woodside-cafe/menus/ on the preview
    build — checked, not assumed. The canonical is deliberately absolute and
    unfiltered: it names the production URL from every build, which is the
    one address a crawler should be told about.

    `to` may also be absolute: a menu on the menu subdomain, as `menuLink()`
    returns it (see legacyRedirects.js). That one is already the production
    URL, so it is used as-is rather than prefixed with the domain.

    The 0-second refresh fires before paint; the link is for anything that
    ignores it. -#}
{%- set dest = legacy.to if '://' in legacy.to else client.domain ~ legacy.to %}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>Redirecting…</title>
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">
    <meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url={{ legacy.to }}">
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{ dest }}">
  </head>
  <body>
    <p>This page has moved to <a href="{{ legacy.to }}">{{ dest }}</a>.</p>
  </body>
</html>
